            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Butter (or curds) primarily symbolizes material abundance, prosperity, and divine provision, with alternative contextual meanings including a subsistence diet after devastation, hospitable provision, or flattering speech. Rare allegorical traditions occasionally associate it with spiritual nourishment or the Word.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Butter symbolizes the Word of God ('milk') that has been tested and worked ('churned') into sound judgment, granting the ability to refuse evil and choose good, and which spiritually cleanses one's walk, with deceptive smooth speech acting as its counterfeit.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Material abundance, prosperity, and divine provision, or alternatively, a subsistence diet.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Tested teaching or the matured Word that produces sound judgment and spiritual cleansing.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes the primary consensus meaning of physical abundance or pastoral prosperity, instead reinterpreting passages like Job 29:6 (washing steps with butter) as spiritual cleansing of the feet (via John 13), while incorporating Psalm 55:21 as a recognized counterfeit (smooth speech).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus views butter as a literal foodstuff symbolizing material conditions (wealth or hardship) based on its agricultural context. The book rejects this material symbolism entirely, constructing a highly specific allegorical definition where butter is the spiritually matured Word (combining Proverbs 30:33 and Isaiah 7:15).\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book diverges from the consensus by replacing the established primary referent of material abundance with a novel spiritual synthesis. It links 'churning milk' (Prov 30) with 'knowing to refuse evil' (Isa 7) to define butter as 'the word worked into sound judgment', and makes an allegorical leap from Job's 'steps washed with butter' to Jesus washing the disciples' feet.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The connection between Job 29:6 ('washed my steps with butter', a clear poetic image of past material prosperity) and John 13 (Jesus washing feet with water) relies on an extreme typological leap. Furthermore, defining butter universally as 'the word worked into sound judgment' ignores the vast majority of its biblical occurrences where it simply denotes pastoral food and material wealth.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
